@@ -24,7 +24,7 @@ runs offline exactly as before.
 | Account | `net_nextendo_apple.m` | OAuth 2.0 authorization code + PKCE in an in-app sheet (iPhone) or the browser (Mac), redirect caught on `127.0.0.1`; refresh token in the Keychain; `GET /api/nex-token` gives the NEX login token and the PID |
 | ID token | `net_nextendo_apple.m`, `hle_account.c` | the network service account's ID token: a JWT shaped like Nintendo BaaS's with the PID as `sub` and the NEX token as `nnex` |
 | Sockets | `dm/host/hle_net.c`, `hle_offline.c` | `nn::socket` on the host's BSD sockets (non-blocking at the host; the game's blocking calls wait in slices). TCP reaches Nextendo only, UDP any address (Pia's peers). Errnos and `O_NONBLOCK` (0x800) are Linux's, as the console's library has them; only the socket options whose number means the same on Horizon and on Apple's kernel reach the host socket, the others are kept and read back as set |
-| nifm | `hle_net.c`, `hle_offline.c` | the network is available; the IP configuration is the host's |
+| nifm | `hle_net.c`, `hle_offline.c` | the network is available; the IP configuration is the host's. A game calls whichever overload its SDK has, so both are hooked for `GetCurrentPrimaryIpAddress` (`nn::socket::InAddr` in `hle_net.c`, `in_addr` in `hle_offline.c`) and for `GetCurrentIpConfigInfo` — each exactly once, since the loader stops a game whose forced-takeover list holds a name twice |
 | NEX login | `dm/games/01006A800016E000/patches.py`, `hle_net.c` | gate `OBS_NEX_LOGIN` on the login job gives NEX's NgsFacade a direct server address, `10.13.37.1:443`, where NEX speaks its WebSocket without TLS |
 | nn::ssl | `hle_net.c` | a connection's handshake on a socket connected to the game server swaps the bridge in for that socket, with the host name the game set; Read, Write, Peek and Poll are then the socket's. This is how Mario Kart 8 Deluxe's NEX client logs in, with no patch of the game |
 | TLS bridge | `net_nextendo_apple.m`, `hle_net.c` | a TCP connection to `10.13.37.1`, or to the game server on the port its station URL names, is a local socket whose other end goes to the server in TLS (Network.framework), the server's key checked against the pins of `DM_NEXTENDO_PIN`, the `Host` header rewritten. Port 443 on the server is the game's own HTTPS and is left plain: `nn::ssl`'s handshake puts the bridge in its place with the name the game set, and if the game writes in clear there instead, its first bytes bring the bridge in |
